rnet Protocol Television)</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/wireless-internet-service-provider">Wireless Internet Service Provider (WISP)</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/wireless-backhaul">Wireless Backhaul</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/define-protocol-in-computer-network">Define Protocol in Computer Network</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/virtual-network-adapter">Virtual Network Adapter</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/virtual-routing-and-forwarding">Virtual Routing and Forwarding</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/virtual-switches">Virtual Switches(vSwitches)</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/vlan-virtual-lan">VLAN (Virtual LAN)</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/wireless-mesh-network-wmn">Wireless Mesh Network (WMN)</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/what-is-gateway-in-computer-network">What is Gateway in Computer Network</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/radio-access-network-ran">Radio Access Network (RAN)</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/what-is-file-sharing">What is File Sharing</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/passive-optical-network-pon">Passive Optical Network (PON)</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/private-ip-address">Private IP address</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/public-key-certificate">Public Key Certificate</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/what-is-bridge-in-computer-network">What is Bridge in Computer Network</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/ping-sweep-icmp-sweep">Ping Sweep (ICMP Sweep)</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/print-server">Print Server</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/wifi-pineapple">WIFI Pineapple</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/walled-garden">Walled Garden</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/wireless-isp">Wireless ISP (wireless Internet service provider or WISP)</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/bridge-vs-repeater">Bridge vs Repeater</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/hardware-security-module-hsm">Hardware Security Module (HSM)</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/public-switched-telephone-network">Public Switched Telephone Network</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/analog-telephone-adapter-ata">Analog Telephone Adapter (ATA)</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/host-bus-adapter-hba">Host Bus Adapter (HBA)</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/iscsi-initiator">ISCSI initiator</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/simplest-protocol">Simplest Protocol</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/telecommunication-networks">Telecommunication Networks</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/what-is-wps-in-wi-fi">What is WPS in Wi-Fi</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/what-are-the-most-important-email-security-protocols">What are the Most Important Email Security Protocols</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/what-is-data-governance-and-why-does-it-matter">What is Data Governance and Why does it Matter</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/carrier-network">Carrier Network</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/most-secure-network-protocol">Most Secure Network Protocol</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/network-smb">Network SMB</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/bgp-vs-eigrp-whats-the-difference">BGP vs. EIGRP: What's the Difference</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/wireless-security-wep-wpa-wpa2-and-wpa3-differences">Wireless Security: WEP, WPA, WPA2 and WPA3 differences</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/cloud-radio-access-network">Cloud Radio Access Network (C-RAN)</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/bits-per-second-bps-or-bit-sec">Bits Per Second (bps or bit/sec)</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/blade-server">Blade Server</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/command-and-control-server">command-and-control server (C&amp;C server)</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/unguided-transmission">UnGuided Transmission</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/ppp-protocol">PPP Protocol</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/bcd-to-seven-segment-decoder">BCD to Seven Segment Decoder</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/spanning-tree-protocol">Spanning Tree Protocol</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/administrative-distance-ad-and-autonomous-system-as">Administrative Distance (AD) and Autonomous System (AS)</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/ethernet-frame-format">Ethernet Frame Format</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/computer-network-aaa-authentication-authorization-and-accounting">Computer Network AAA (Authentication, Authorization and Accounting)</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/integrated-services-digital-network">Integrated Services Digital Network</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/frame-relay-in-computer-network">Frame relay in Computer Network</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/aaa-authentication-authorization-and-accounting-configuration-locally">AAA (Authentication, Authorization and Accounting) configuration (locally)</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/back-off-algorithm-for-csma-cd">Back-off Algorithm for CSMA/CD</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/difference-between-byte-stuffing-and-bit-stuffing">Difference between Byte stuffing and Bit stuffing</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/encryption-vs-digest-in-cryptography">Encryption vs Digest in Cryptography</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/how-does-symmetric-and-asymmetric-key-cryptography-works-together">How does Symmetric and Asymmetric Key Cryptography Works Together</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/inter-switch-link-isl-and-ieee-802-1q">Inter-Switch Link (ISL) and IEEE 802.1Q</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/port-security-in-computer-network">Port security in Computer Network</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/program-to-remotely-power-on-a-pc-over-the-internet-using-the-wake-on-lan-protocol">Program to remotely Power on a PC over the internet using the Wake-on-LAN protocol</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/rfc">RFC (Request For Comment)</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/ssd-caching">SSD Caching</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/tetra-terrestrial-trunked-radio">TETRA(Terrestrial Trunked Radio)</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/what-are-zombie-cookies-and-can-you-block-them">What are Zombie Cookies, and can you Block them</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/what-is-application-security-posture-management">What is Application Security Posture Management (ASPM)</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/what-is-bug-bounty-program">What is Bug Bounty Program</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/what-is-clampi-virus">What is Clampi Virus</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/what-is-the-risk-based-vulnerability-management">What is the Risk-Based Vulnerability Management</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/wireless-network-roaming-and-handover-protocols">Wireless Network Roaming and Handover Protocols</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/what-is-the-stateless-protocol">What is the Stateless Protocol</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/mac-layer-functions-and-protocol">Mac Layer Functions and Protocol</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/advantages-of-written-communication">Advantages of Written Communication</a></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="accordion-item" style="background-color:var(--sidebar)"><h2 class="accordion-header" id="flush-heading11"><button aria-controls="flush-collapse11" aria-expanded="true" class="accordion-button sidebar-btn" data-bs-target="#flush-collapse11" data-bs-toggle="collapse" type="button">MCQ</button></h2><div aria-labelledby="flush-heading11" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" id="flush-collapse11"><div class="accordion-body"><ul><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/computer-network-mcq">Computer Network MCQ</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/computer-network-mcq-part2">Computer Network MCQ Part2</a></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="accordion-item" style="background-color:var(--sidebar)"><h2 class="accordion-header" id="flush-heading12"><button aria-controls="flush-collapse12" aria-expanded="true" class="accordion-button sidebar-btn" data-bs-target="#flush-collapse12" data-bs-toggle="collapse" type="button">Interview Questions</button></h2><div aria-labelledby="flush-heading12" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" id="flush-collapse12"><div class="accordion-body"><ul><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/networking-interview-questions">Networking Questions</a></li></ul></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="col-12 col-md-7"><div class="overview"><div id="city"><table><tbody><tr><td><div id="bottomnextup"><a class="next" href="computer-network-introduction">next →</a></div><h1 class="h1">Learn Computer Network Tutorial</h1><div style="font-size:14px;margin-top:5px;margin-bottom:5px;color:#6f6e6e"><span>22 Aug 2025</span> | <span><i aria-hidden="true" class="fa fa-clock-o"></i> 5 min read</span></div><img alt="Computer Network Tutorial" src="https://images.tpointtech.com/tutorial/computer-network/images/computer-network-tutorial-1.png"><p>Computer Network tutorial provides basic and advanced concepts of Data Communication &amp; Networks (DCN). Our Computer Networking Tutorial is designed for beginners and professionals.</p><p>Our Computer Network tutorial includes all topics of Computer Network such as introduction, features, types of computer network, architecture, hardware, software, internet, intranet, website, LAN, WAN, etc.</p><h2 class="h2">What is Computer Network?</h2><p>A computer network is a set of devices connected through links. A node can be computer, printer, or any other device capable of sending or receiving the data. The links connecting the nodes are known as communication channels.</p><p>Computer Network uses distributed processing in which task is divided among several computers. Instead of a single computer handling an entire task, each separate computer handles a subset." This aligns with the correct understanding of distributed processing, where multiple computers work on different parts of a task.</p><h3 class="h3">Some Benefits of Computer Network:</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Security:</strong> It provides limited interaction that a user can have with the entire system. For example, a bank allows the users to access their own accounts through an ATM without allowing them to access the bank's entire database.</li><li><strong>Data Sharing:</strong> Collaboration among teams on shared documents, apps, or systems increases productivity.</li><li><strong>Web email, video, and instant messaging communication:</strong> Networks allow for both delayed and real-time communication. Information is accessible, messages can be sent, video calls can be made, and chat is available.</li><li><strong>Data management:</strong> Networks enable businesses to store data in a distributed or central location, which facilitates the management, security, and backup of important data.</li><li><strong>Remote access:</strong> Remote access allows users to reach computers, servers, or cloud platforms from various locations, enabling round-the-clock access and remote work.</li></ul><div class="index"><h2 class="h2">Computer Network Basics</h2><ul class="points"><li><a href="computer-network-tutorial">Computer Network Tutorial</a></li><li><a href="computer-network-introduction">Computer Network Introduction</a></li><li><a href="computer-network-features">Computer Network Features</a></li><li><a href="computer-network-architecture">Computer Network Architecture</a></li><li><a href="computer-network-components">Computer Network Components</a></li><li><a href="types-of-computer-network">Computer Network Types</a></li><li><a href="computer-network-topologies">Computer Network Topologies</a></li><li><a href="computer-network-transmission-modes">Computer Network Transmission Modes</a></li></ul><h2 class="h2">Computer Network Models</h2><p>Including software, firmware, chip-level engineering hardware, and electrical signals, networking engineering is a complicated process. The entire networking concept is divided into several layers to make network engineering easier to understand.</p><p>Every layer functions independently of the others and is responsible for completing a specific task. However, these layers are essential to almost every networking function taken together. To process input and output, the layers only depend on one another and exchange data.</p><ul class="points"><li><a href="computer-network-models">Computer Network Models</a></li><li><a href="osi-model">OSI Model</a></li><li><a href="computer-network-tcp-ip-model">TCP/IP Model</a></li></ul><h2 class="h2">Physical Layer</h2><p>Data transmission, both electrical and physical, is handled by the physical layer, which is the bottom layer in the Open System Interconnection (OSI) Model. It is made up of a variety of network components, including receiver connectors, power plugs, and cable types.</p><p>Data bits are sent between devices (such as computers) via the physical layer. The types of encoding or how the 0s and 1s are encoded in a signal are defined by the physical layer. It is responsible for transmitting unstructured raw data streams via a tangible medium.</p><ul class="points"><li><a href="physical-layer-in-osi-model">Physical Layer</a></li><li><a href="computer-network-topologies">Network Topology</a></li><li><a href="computer-network-digital-transmission">Digital Transmission</a></li><li><a href="transmission-media">Transmission Media</a></li><li><a href="guided-transmission-media">Guided Media</a></li><li><a href="unguided-transmission-media">UnGuided Media</a></li><li><a href="multiplexing-in-computer-network">Multiplexing</a></li><li><a href="multiplexing-techniques">Multiplexing Techniques</a></li><li><a href="computer-network-switching">Switching</a></li><li><a href="computer-network-switching-modes">Switching Modes</a></li><li><a href="computer-network-switching-techniques">Switching Techniques</a></li></ul><h2 class="h2">Data Link Layer</h2><p>The main focus of the data link layer is to provide dependable data transfer between devices that are directly connected. In this layer, you will learn about data framing, identifying and fixing transmission errors, controlling data flow, and different switching strategies.</p><ul class="points"><li><a href="data-link-layer">Data Link layer</a></li><li><a href="computer-network-error-detection">Error Detection</a></li><li><a href="computer-network-error-correction">Error Correction</a></li><li><a href="data-link-controls">Data Link Controls</a></li><li><a href="sliding-window-protocol">Sliding Window Protocol</a></li></ul><h2 class="h2">Network Layer</h2><p>The logical addressing and routing of data packets across various interconnected networks is covered in this section. You'll learn about IP addressing schemes, including IPv4 and IPv6, and the differences between private and public addresses.</p><ul class="points"><li><a href="network-layer">Network Layer</a></li><li><a href="network-addressing">Network Addressing</a></li><li><a href="computer-network-routing">Computer Network Routing</a></li><li><a href="network-layer-protocols">Network Layer Protocols</a></li><li><a href="ipv4-header-in-computer-networks">Ipv4 Header</a></li><li><a href="ipv4-vs-ipv6">IPv4 vs IPv6</a></li><li><a href="public-and-private-ip-addresses">Public and Private IP Addresses</a></li></ul><h2 class="h2">Routing Algorithm</h2><p>Computer networks are fundamentally based on routing algorithms. They would be necessary for data to move between various network segments. The rules that determine how data is sent through a network are known as routing algorithms.</p><p>Each of the many routing algorithms has advantages and disadvantages of its own. It is crucial to choose the appropriate routing algorithm for a network because it may have a major impact on the network's functionality.</p><ul class="points"><li><a href="computer-network-routing-algorithm">Routing Algorithm</a></li><li><a href="distance-vector-routing-algorithm">Distance Vector Routing Algorithm</a></li><li><a href="link-state-routing-algorithm">Link State Routing Algorithm</a></li></ul><h2 class="h2">Transport Layer</h2><p>This section explains how applications can communicate across a network. Let's discuss the two primary transport layer protocols TCP and UDP, and examine how they manage data flow, establish connections, check for errors, and handle network congestion.</p><ul class="points"><li><a href="computer-network-transport-layer">Transport Layer</a></li><li><a href="computer-network-transport-layer-protocols">Transport Layer Protocols</a></li><li><a href="tcp">TCP</a></li><li><a href="tcp-connection-termination">Tcp Termination</a></li><li><a href="udp-protocol">UDP Protocol</a></li><li><a href="tcp-vs-udp">TCP vs UDP</a></li></ul><h2 class="h2">Session and Presentation Layer</h2><p>The upper layers are displayed in a format to manage communication dialogues (sessions) and to ensure that it is compatible in this section. The capabilities of the Session Layer, as well as the Presentation Layer's data translation, encryption, and compression services, will be covered.</p><ul class="points"><li><a href="session-layer-in-osi-model">Session Layer</a></li><li><a href="presentation-layer-in-osi-model">Presentation Layer</a></li><li><a href="transport-layer-security-secure-socket-layer-ssl-and-ssl-architecture">Secure Socket Layer (SSL)</a></li></ul><h2 class="h2">Application Layer</h2><p>In the Open Systems Interconnection (OSI) communications model, the application layer is located at Layer 7, which is the topmost layer. It ensures that a program can interact with other programs on various networks and computer systems in an efficient manner.</p><p>An application is distinct from the application layer. Rather, it is an element of an application that manages how other devices communicate with it. The remaining part of the application is hidden from the transmission process by this abstraction layer service.</p><p>To finish its process, the application layer depends on every layer. At this point, the application or data is shown to the user in an understandable visual format.</p><ul class="points"><li><a href="computer-network-application-layer">Application Layer</a></li><li><a href="computer-network-client-and-server-model">Client and Server Model</a></li></ul><h2 class="h2">Application Protocols</h2><p>It is essential to understand application layer protocols for effective communication and data transfer in computer networks. Application layer protocols are a set of protocols that function at the top layer of the OSI model and facilitate communication between applications running on different devices.</p><p>Application layer protocols specify how programs on various devices communicate with one another across a network. Application layer protocols that are commonly used include HTTP, FTP, SMTP, POP, TELNET, TFTP, LDAP, and DNS.</p><p>The rules and specifications controlling the transmission and receiving of messages between various devices on a network are specific to each protocol.</p><ul class="points"><li><a href="computer-network-dns">DNS</a></li><li><a href="computer-network-ftp">FTP</a></li><li><a href="computer-network-telnet">Telnet</a></li><li><a href="simple-mail-transfer-protocol">SMTP</a></li><li><a href="simple-network-management-protocol">SNMP</a></li><li><a href="computer-network-http">HTTP</a></li></ul><h2 class="h2">Cloud networking</h2><p>We will study cloud networking to understand how networking concepts are modified and used in modern cloud computing environments. This section discusses Network Function Virtualization (NFV), the use of Virtual Private Networks (VPNs) for secure cloud access, and various cloud service delivery models.</p><ul class="points"><li><a href="cloud-networking-managing-and-optimizing-cloud-based-networks">Cloud Networking</a></li><li><a href="cloud-service-models">Types of Cloud Services</a></li><li><a href="what-is-vpn-and-how-it-works">Virtual Private Network</a></li><li><a href="network-function-virtualization-nfv-transforming-network-architecture-with-virtualized-functions">Network Function Virtualization (NFV)</a></li></ul><h2 class="h2">Developing Networking Trends</h2><p>In the last section, we will examine networking technology's future. Well, quickly go over some of the innovative advancements influencing the next generation of networks, like 5G mobile technology and the adaptable network slicing capabilities.</p><h2 class="h2">Computer Network Security</h2><ul class="points"><li><a href="computer-network-security">Security</a></li><li><a href="computer-network-privacy">Privacy</a></li><li><a href="computer-network-digital-signature">Digital Signature</a></li><li><a href="computer-network-pgp">PGP</a></li></ul><h2 class="h2">Interview Questions</h2><ul class="points"><li><a href="networking-interview-questions">Computer Network Interview Questions</a></li></ul><h2 class="h2">MCQ</h2><ul class="points"><li><a href="computer-network-mcq">Computer Network MCQ</a></li></ul></div><h2 class="h2">Prerequisite</h2><p>There is nothing specific prerequisite for learning computer network.</p><h2 class="h2">Audience</h2><p>Our Computer Network Tutorial is designed to help beginners and professionals.</p><h2 class="h2">Problems</h2><p>We assure that you will not find any problem in this Computer Network Tutorial. But if there is any mistake, please post the problem in contact form.</p><hr/><div class="nexttopicdiv"><span class="nexttopictext">Next Topic</span><span class="nexttopiclink"><a href="computer-network-introduction">Introduction</a></span></div> <div id="bottomnext"><a class="next" href="computer-network-introduction" style="float:right">next →</a></div><br> </br></img></td></tr></tbody></table></div><br/></div><div class="related-posts-container"><h2 class="h3">Related Posts</h2><ul class="related-posts-list"><li><a class="related-post-item" href="computer-network-introduction"><h3 class="h3">What is a Computer Network?</h3><p class="related-post-content">Computer Network is a group of computers connected with each other through wires, optical fibres or optical links so that various devices can interact with each other through a network. The aim of the computer network is the sharing of resources among various devices. In the case...</p><p class="reading-time"><i aria-hidden="true" class="fa fa-clock-o"></i> 12 min read <span class="arrow-position"><svg fill="currentColor" height="1.3rem" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewbox="0 0 512 512" width="1.3rem" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M295.6 163.7c-5.1 5-5.1 13.3-.1 18.4l60.8 60.9H124.9c-7.1 0-12.9 5.8-12.9 13s5.8 13 12.9 13h231.3l-60.8 60.9c-5 5.1-4.9 13.3.1 18.4 5.1 5 13.2 5 18.3-.1l82.4-83c1.1-1.2 2-2.5 2.7-4.1.7-1.6 1-3.3 1-5 0-3.4-1.3-6.6-3.7-9.1l-82.4-83c-4.9-5.2-13.1-5.3-18.2-.3z"></path></svg></span></p></a></li><li><a class="related-post-item" href="computer-network-topologies"><h3 class="h3">Topologies</h3><p class="related-post-content">What is Network Topology? Topology defines the structure of the network of how all the components are interconnected to each other. It refers to the physical or logical arrangement of computers, cables, and other devices in a computer network. There are two types of topology: physical...</p><p class="reading-time"><i aria-hidden="true" class="fa fa-clock-o"></i> 9 min read <span class="arrow-position"><svg fill="currentColor" height="1.3rem" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewbox="0 0 512 512" width="1.3rem" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M295.6 163.7c-5.1 5-5.1 13.3-.1 18.4l60.8 60.9H124.9c-7.1 0-12.9 5.8-12.9 13s5.8 13 12.9 13h231.3l-60.8 60.9c-5 5.1-4.9 13.3.1 18.4 5.1 5 13.2 5 18.3-.1l82.4-83c1.1-1.2 2-2.5 2.7-4.1.7-1.6 1-3.3 1-5 0-3.4-1.3-6.6-3.7-9.1l-82.4-83c-4.9-5.2-13.1-5.3-18.2-.3z"></path></svg></span></p></a></li><li><a class="related-post-item" href="computer-network-architecture"><h3 class="h3">Computer Netw